{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nAfter repository extraction, `BindingStore` still owns forward/reverse identity transitions, pending/confirmed state, stale reverse cleanup, and immutable numeric-ID moves. These coupled rules need one policy owner and both-index tests.\n\n## Outcome\nCreate the first `BindingLifecycle` slice for bind, confirm, unbind, reverse cleanup, and numeric-ID re-key while the public facade retains all signatures and owns coordination.\n\n## Plan\n1. Start with table-driven failing tests over valid, missing, corrupt, duplicate, and stale forward/reverse combinations.\n2. Move the cohesive identity transition call graph into `binding_lifecycle.py`, operating on repository-owned original dictionaries.\n3. Preserve the EXISTING behaviors exactly: targeted stale reverse removal (enumerated below), pending/confirmed semantics, immutable numeric-ID re-key, and legacy no-ID behavior. There is no ambiguity abort on the reviewed base and this slice does not introduce one.\n4. Delegate existing facade methods without exposing the lifecycle owner or repository to adapters.\n\n## Testing\nReload through the real repository after every transition and assert both indexes plus exact caller-visible outcomes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every valid bind/confirm/unbind transition leaves at most one forward and one matching reverse identity.\n- [ ] `bind_confirm` remains last-writer-wins on the forward entry and pops EXACTLY the one differing old reverse key, with no sweep, no ambiguity detection, and no abort - the reviewed base has no such abort, so this slice must not add one.\n- [ ] Stale reverse entries are removed only in the existing permitted cases.\n- [ ] Immutable numeric-ID matches re-key moved issues instead of retiring them.\n- [ ] Legacy records without numeric IDs retain current behavior and facade signatures.\n\n## Out of scope\nAbsence/tombstone/comment policy and rich-emission mutation move to later S2 tasks.\n\n## Extraction seam for `note_absent_or_rekey` (resolves the S2 T1 scope split)\n`note_absent_or_rekey` mixes this task's numeric-ID re-key rule with the absence/tombstone\npath that belongs to S2 T2, so the seam is named explicitly rather than left to the\nimplementer. On the reviewed base the method is:\n\n1. guard - resolve `local_id` + entry from the reverse index; return `False` if either is missing;\n2. no `client` or no captured `jira_id` -> `self.note_absent(jira_key)`; return `False`;\n3. `_current_key_by_id` returns nothing, or the same key -> `self.note_absent(jira_key)`; return `False`;\n4. a DIFFERENT current key -> re-key: set the entry's `jira_key`, reset `absent_404_count`, stamp\n   `updated_at`, pop the old reverse key, insert the new one, `save()`, emit `binding-rekeyed`.\n\nTHIS TASK extracts only step 4 (plus the step-3 identity comparison that decides it) into\n`binding_lifecycle.py` as a named re-key operation returning whether a re-key occurred.\n`note_absent_or_rekey` REMAINS on the facade as the coordinator: it asks the lifecycle owner\nto attempt a re-key and, on a negative result, falls through to `self.note_absent(...)`, which\nstays facade-owned until S2 T2 moves absence/retirement policy. The public signature and the\nTrue/False return contract do not change, and no branch changes its outcome - so\n`note_absent` is called in exactly the same cases, the same number of times, as before.\n\n## Permitted stale-reverse removals (enumerates AC3's \"existing permitted cases\")\nReverse entries may be removed ONLY in these existing cases; any other removal is a guessed\ndeletion and must safety-abort instead:\n- `bind_confirm` rebind: the entry already held a different `jira_key`, so the OLD key's\n  reverse entry is popped in the same operation (otherwise it dangles at this local id forever);\n- `unbind`: the keyed reverse entry is popped, then any reverse key still pointing at this\n  `local_id` is swept (this is the arm `bridge fsck --repair` prune relies on, including keys\n  orphaned out of band);\n- re-key (step 4 above): the old key is popped as the new key is inserted, in one operation;\n- `_retire`: the retired key's reverse entry is popped as the binding moves to the retired file.\n\n\n## Correction: no ambiguity abort exists on the reviewed base\nAn earlier draft of this task asserted an existing \"safety-abort on ambiguous double bind\" to be\npreserved. Verified against the code: none exists. `bind_confirm` unconditionally overwrites the\nforward entry's `jira_key`, then pops the old reverse key only when it differs; there is no\nambiguity detection, no refusal, and no raise. `unbind` and the re-key path likewise never abort.\n\nThe real invariant this task preserves is therefore NARROWER and is what the tests assert: every\ntransition leaves at most one forward entry and one matching reverse entry, achieved by targeted\nsingle-key pops rather than by detecting or refusing ambiguity. Adding an abort would be a\nBEHAVIOR CHANGE, which a pure extraction slice is forbidden from making; if an abort is wanted it\nbelongs in its own ticket with its own risk analysis. The safety-abort language that does apply to\nthis epic is S3's interrupted-retirement completion, which is genuinely new behavior there.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787114774590002001,"uuid":"1ec8acd3-72c9-4b69-bbd6-9e1720b47c6c"}